pg:CO1 a ipo:LearningObjective, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "Describe a color as an RGBA tuple, look up names with ImageColor, and locate pixels by (x, y) and rectangles by box tuples"@en ;
    skos:definition "The objective is met when the reader can describe a color as an RGBA tuple, look up names with ImageColor, and locate pixels by (x, y) and rectangles by box tuples, at the apply level."@en ; dcterms:source <https://automatetheboringstuff.com/3e/chapter21.html> ;
    ld:bloomLevel ld:Apply  .
pg:CO2 a ipo:LearningObjective, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "Open, create, inspect and save images with Pillow, and choose a file format that can hold the image's mode"@en ;
    skos:definition "The objective is met when the reader can open, create, inspect and save images with Pillow, and choose a file format that can hold the image's mode, at the apply level."@en ; dcterms:source <https://automatetheboringstuff.com/3e/chapter21.html> ;
    ld:bloomLevel ld:Apply  .
pg:CO3 a ipo:LearningObjective, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "Crop, copy, paste, resize, rotate, flip and set pixels, telling which calls return a new image and which change their image in place"@en ;
    skos:definition "The objective is met when the reader can crop, copy, paste, resize, rotate, flip and set pixels, telling which calls return a new image and which change their image in place, at the apply level."@en ; dcterms:source <https://automatetheboringstuff.com/3e/chapter21.html> ;
    ld:bloomLevel ld:Apply  .
pg:CO4 a ipo:LearningObjective, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "Analyze the batch resize-and-logo program, its test, its arithmetic and its file filter, and find what it skips or gets wrong"@en ;
    skos:definition "The objective is met when the reader can analyze the batch resize-and-logo program, its test, its arithmetic and its file filter, and find what it skips or gets wrong, at the analyze level."@en ; dcterms:source <https://automatetheboringstuff.com/3e/chapter21.html> ;
    ld:bloomLevel ld:Analyze  .
pg:CO5 a ipo:LearningObjective, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "Draw shapes and text with ImageDraw and predict which pixels a box draws"@en ;
    skos:definition "The objective is met when the reader can draw shapes and text with ImageDraw and predict which pixels a box draws, at the apply level."@en ; dcterms:source <https://automatetheboringstuff.com/3e/chapter21.html> ;
    ld:bloomLevel ld:Apply  .
pg:CO6 a ipo:LearningObjective, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "Make line, scatter, bar and pie graphs with Matplotlib, save them with savefig, and explain what pyplot keeps as current state"@en ;
    skos:definition "The objective is met when the reader can make line, scatter, bar and pie graphs with Matplotlib, save them with savefig, and explain what pyplot keeps as current state, at the apply level."@en ; dcterms:source <https://automatetheboringstuff.com/3e/chapter21.html> ;
    ld:bloomLevel ld:Apply  .
pg:Q1 a ld:AssessmentItem, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "A new image is made with Image.new('RGBA', (10, 10)) and no colour. What does getpixel((0, 0)) return?"@en ; ld:hasOption pg:Q1_O0, pg:Q1_O1, pg:Q1_O2 ; ld:hasCorrectOption pg:Q1_O1 ; ld:cognitiveLevel ld:Apply ; ld:assessesObjective pg:CO1 ;
    ld:distractorRationale "Opaque black is the default of an RGB image; an RGBA image starts with alpha 0." ;
    ld:distractorRationale "Pillow does not start from white." .
pg:Q1_O0 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "(0, 0, 0, 255), opaque black" .
pg:Q1_O1 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "(0, 0, 0, 0), invisible" .
pg:Q1_O2 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "(255, 255, 255, 255), opaque white" .
pg:Q2 a ld:AssessmentItem, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "Table 21-1 of the chapter gives Green as (0, 255, 0, 255). What does ImageColor.getcolor('green', 'RGBA') return?"@en ; ld:hasOption pg:Q2_O0, pg:Q2_O1, pg:Q2_O2 ; ld:hasCorrectOption pg:Q2_O1 ; ld:cognitiveLevel ld:Understand ; ld:assessesObjective pg:CO1 ;
    ld:distractorRationale "That is the colour of 'lime', the HTML name of full green; the table has one wrong row." ;
    ld:distractorRationale "'green' is in Pillow's table of 148 names." .
pg:Q2_O0 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "(0, 255, 0, 255), as the table says" .
pg:Q2_O1 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "(0, 128, 0, 255); the table's value belongs to 'lime'" .
pg:Q2_O2 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "It raises ValueError" .
pg:Q3 a ld:AssessmentItem, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "What is the size of im.crop((10, 5, 40, 35)) for a 60 by 40 image?"@en ; ld:hasOption pg:Q3_O0, pg:Q3_O1, pg:Q3_O2 ; ld:hasCorrectOption pg:Q3_O1 ; ld:cognitiveLevel ld:Apply ; ld:assessesObjective pg:CO1 ;
    ld:distractorRationale "A box includes its left and top edge and excludes its right and bottom edge." ;
    ld:distractorRationale "The box tuple holds corners, not a size: the width is 40 minus 10." .
pg:Q3_O0 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "(31, 31), because both edges are included" .
pg:Q3_O1 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "(30, 30), because the right and bottom edges are left out" .
pg:Q3_O2 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "(40, 35), the box's last corner" .
pg:Q4 a ld:AssessmentItem, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "An RGBA image is saved with im.save('card.jpg'). What happens?"@en ; ld:hasOption pg:Q4_O0, pg:Q4_O1, pg:Q4_O2 ; ld:hasCorrectOption pg:Q4_O1 ; ld:cognitiveLevel ld:Apply ; ld:assessesObjective pg:CO2 ;
    ld:distractorRationale "JPEG has no alpha channel, so there is nothing to keep it in." ;
    ld:distractorRationale "Pillow chooses the format from the extension and does not rename it." .
pg:Q4_O0 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "A JPEG is written and the transparency is kept" .
pg:Q4_O1 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "An OSError, 'cannot write mode RGBA as JPEG'" .
pg:Q4_O2 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "The file is written as a PNG with the .jpg name" .
pg:Q5 a ld:AssessmentItem, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "What does a.paste(b, (0, 0)) return, and what has happened to a?"@en ; ld:hasOption pg:Q5_O0, pg:Q5_O1, pg:Q5_O2 ; ld:hasCorrectOption pg:Q5_O1 ; ld:cognitiveLevel ld:Analyze ; ld:assessesObjective pg:CO3 ;
    ld:distractorRationale "crop() and resize() return new images, but paste() works on its own image." ;
    ld:distractorRationale "paste() returns nothing; it copies b into a." .
pg:Q5_O0 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "A new image that holds the result; a is unchanged" .
pg:Q5_O1 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "None; a itself has been changed" .
pg:Q5_O2 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "a copy of b" .
pg:Q6 a ld:AssessmentItem, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "A white RGB image is rotated with im.rotate(30). What colour are the corners that no pixel reaches?"@en ; ld:hasOption pg:Q6_O0, pg:Q6_O1, pg:Q6_O2 ; ld:hasCorrectOption pg:Q6_O1 ; ld:cognitiveLevel ld:Analyze ; ld:assessesObjective pg:CO3 ;
    ld:distractorRationale "The fill does not copy the picture's colour." ;
    ld:distractorRationale "Transparent is the fill of an RGBA image; an RGB image has no alpha, so the fill is black." .
pg:Q6_O0 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "White, the colour of the picture" .
pg:Q6_O1 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "Black, (0, 0, 0), unless fillcolor is given" .
pg:Q6_O2 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "Transparent, (0, 0, 0, 0)" .
pg:Q7 a ld:AssessmentItem, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "The batch program skips every name that does not end in the lower case .png or .jpg. Which of a.png, C.PNG, d.jpeg and n.txt does it process?"@en ; ld:hasOption pg:Q7_O0, pg:Q7_O1, pg:Q7_O2 ; ld:hasCorrectOption pg:Q7_O0 ; ld:cognitiveLevel ld:Analyze ; ld:assessesObjective pg:CO4 ;
    ld:distractorRationale "The test is case sensitive, so C.PNG is skipped." ;
    ld:distractorRationale "The extension .jpeg is not .jpg, so d.jpeg is skipped as well." .
pg:Q7_O0 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "Only a.png" .
pg:Q7_O1 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "a.png and C.PNG" .
pg:Q7_O2 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "a.png, C.PNG and d.jpeg" .
pg:Q8 a ld:AssessmentItem, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "draw.rectangle((20, 30, 60, 60), fill='red') is called on a black image. How many red pixels are there?"@en ; ld:hasOption pg:Q8_O0, pg:Q8_O1, pg:Q8_O2 ; ld:hasCorrectOption pg:Q8_O1 ; ld:cognitiveLevel ld:Apply ; ld:assessesObjective pg:CO5 ;
    ld:distractorRationale "That is the crop() arithmetic; a drawn rectangle includes its last pixel." ;
    ld:distractorRationale "Unlike crop(), ImageDraw includes the last pixel." .
pg:Q8_O0 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "1200, which is 40 times 30" .
pg:Q8_O1 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "1271, which is 41 times 31" .
pg:Q8_O2 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "1271 is wrong; the last pixel is left out, so 1160" .
pg:Q9 a ld:AssessmentItem, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "The 2nd edition used draw.textsize() to measure text. Which calls does Pillow 12 offer instead?"@en ; ld:hasOption pg:Q9_O0, pg:Q9_O1, pg:Q9_O2 ; ld:hasCorrectOption pg:Q9_O1 ; ld:cognitiveLevel ld:Understand ; ld:assessesObjective pg:CO5 ;
    ld:distractorRationale "It was removed in Pillow 10.0.0." ;
    ld:distractorRationale "getsize() of fonts was removed in the same release." .
pg:Q9_O0 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "textsize() still exists in Pillow 12" .
pg:Q9_O1 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "textbbox() and textlength()" .
pg:Q9_O2 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "getsize(), on the image" .
pg:Q10 a ld:AssessmentItem, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "What does plt.plot([1, 2, 3], [1, 4, 9]) return?"@en ; ld:hasOption pg:Q10_O0, pg:Q10_O1, pg:Q10_O2 ; ld:hasCorrectOption pg:Q10_O0 ; ld:cognitiveLevel ld:Apply ; ld:assessesObjective pg:CO6 ;
    ld:distractorRationale "The figure is reached with plt.gcf(); plot() returns the lines it drew." ;
    ld:distractorRationale "plot() draws and returns the lines; show() is the one that returns None." .
pg:Q10_O0 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "A list with one Line2D" .
pg:Q10_O1 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "The figure" .
pg:Q10_O2 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "None" .
pg:Q11 a ld:AssessmentItem, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "In one program plt.plot() is called twice and then plt.show() with the Agg backend. How many lines does plt.gca() hold afterwards?"@en ; ld:hasOption pg:Q11_O0, pg:Q11_O1, pg:Q11_O2 ; ld:hasCorrectOption pg:Q11_O1 ; ld:cognitiveLevel ld:Analyze ; ld:assessesObjective pg:CO6 ;
    ld:distractorRationale "With a window, closing it ends the figure; Agg shows nothing and leaves the figure." ;
    ld:distractorRationale "Every plot call adds to the current axes." .
pg:Q11_O0 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "0, because show() clears the figure" .
pg:Q11_O1 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "2, because pyplot keeps the current figure and show() does not clear it with Agg" .
pg:Q11_O2 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "1, because the second call replaces the first" .
pg:ImageFundamentals a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Image fundamentals"@en ;
    skos:definition "The first part of the chapter, Computer Image Fundamentals, teaches the two ideas every Pillow call rests on: a colour is an RGBA value of four integers from 0 to 255, and a pixel is addressed by an x and y coordinate from the top-left origin, with box tuples naming rectangles (Sweigart, 2025). It then loads zophie.png as an Image object with size, format and save (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch21#S_ImageFundamentals> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ImageFundamentals" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 1 .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ImageFundamentals .
pg:ColorRepresentation a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Color representation"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter explains an RGBA value as red, green, blue and alpha from 0 to 255, gives Table 21-1 of eight colour names, and says Pillow uses the same standard names as HTML and holds more than 100 of them in ImageColor.colormap (Sweigart, 2025). The Pillow ImageColor page says the module converts CSS3-style colour specifiers and provides some 140 standard names, case insensitive, and the CSS Color module lists lime and green as separate colours (Pillow Developers, 2026)(World Wide Web Consortium, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch21#S_ColorRepresentation> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ColorRepresentation" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:ImageFundamentals .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ColorRepresentation .
pg:RgbaTuple a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Rgba tuple"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says Pillow writes red as (255, 0, 0, 255), that alpha 0 means invisible and that the other three numbers then do not matter (Sweigart, 2025). The Pillow concepts page says an image has one or more bands, a PNG for example R, G, B and A, and that the mode is a string that defines the type and depth of a pixel (Pillow Developers, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch21#S_RgbaTuple> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-RgbaTuple" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:ColorRepresentation .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:RgbaTuple .
pg:ColorNames a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Color names"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter calls getcolor() with 'red', 'RED', 'chocolate' and 'CornflowerBlue' and lists the dictionary from 'aliceblue' to 'yellowgreen' (Sweigart, 2025). The Pillow page says its names come from the X Window system and most web browsers, and the CSS Color module lists lime as the colour with full green (Pillow Developers, 2026)(World Wide Web Consortium, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch21#S_ColorNames> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ColorNames" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:ColorRepresentation .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ColorNames .
pg:ImageModes a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Image modes"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter creates every image in mode 'RGBA' and says there are other modes the book does not go into (Sweigart, 2025). The Pillow concepts page describes the mode as the string that defines the type and depth of a pixel (Pillow Developers, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch21#S_ImageModes> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ImageModes" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:ColorRepresentation .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ImageModes .
pg:CoordinateSystem a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Coordinate system"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter teaches the pixel origin at the top-left corner with y growing downward, which it calls the opposite of school mathematics, and the box tuple of left, top, right and bottom with Figure 21-1 and Figure 21-2 (Sweigart, 2025). Pillow's tutorial uses the same box for crop() and paste() (Pillow Developers, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch21#S_CoordinateSystem> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-CoordinateSystem" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:ImageFundamentals .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:CoordinateSystem .
pg:PixelOrigin a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Pixel origin"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says (0, 0) is the top-left pixel, that x grows to the right, that y grows downward and that this bears repeating because it differs from mathematics (Sweigart, 2025). The Pillow reference gives getpixel() the coordinate as (x, y) (Pillow Developers, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch21#S_PixelOrigin> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-PixelOrigin" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:CoordinateSystem .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:PixelOrigin .
pg:BoxTuple a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Box tuple"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter defines the box as left, top, right and bottom, says right and bottom are one pixel beyond the edge, and shows (3, 1, 9, 6) in Figure 21-2 (Sweigart, 2025). Appendix B answer 3 says the four integers are left, top, width and height, which contradicts it (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch21#S_BoxTuple> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-BoxTuple" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:CoordinateSystem .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:BoxTuple .
pg:HalfOpenBox a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Half open box"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter notes that a box includes the left column and top row but not the right column and bottom row (Sweigart, 2025). The Pillow reference calls the crop box a 4-tuple of the left, upper, right and lower pixel coordinate (Pillow Developers, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch21#S_HalfOpenBox> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-HalfOpenBox" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:CoordinateSystem .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:HalfOpenBox .
pg:ImageObject a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Image object"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter loads zophie.png with Image.open(), reads size, filename, format and format_description, saves a JPEG, and makes blank images with Image.new() (Sweigart, 2025). The Pillow tutorial describes the same attributes (Pillow Developers, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch21#S_ImageObject> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ImageObject" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:ImageFundamentals .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ImageObject .
pg:OpenAndSize a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Open and size"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter prints cat_im.size as (816, 1088), format as 'PNG' and format_description as 'Portable network graphics', and unpacks size into width and height (Sweigart, 2025). The Pillow reference says the image data is not read from the file until you try to process it or call load() (Pillow Developers, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch21#S_OpenAndSize> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-OpenAndSize" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:ImageObject .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:OpenAndSize .
pg:ImageFilename a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Image filename"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter prints cat_im.filename as 'zophie.png' and describes it as the original file's name (Sweigart, 2025). The Pillow reference describes Image.open() as taking a filename string, a path object or a file object (Pillow Developers, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch21#S_ImageFilename> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ImageFilename" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:ImageObject .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ImageFilename .
pg:NewImage a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "New image"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter makes a 100 by 200 purple image, calls Image.new() again with no colour for a 20 by 20 image and says the default is the invisible black (0, 0, 0, 0) (Sweigart, 2025). The Pillow reference says the colour default of Image.new() is black and that colour strings are accepted for RGB images (Pillow Developers, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch21#S_NewImage> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-NewImage" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:ImageObject .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:NewImage .
pg:SaveFormats a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Save formats"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter saves zophie.png as zophie.jpg and says Pillow sees the extension .jpg and uses the JPEG format, and that the two files are not identical (Sweigart, 2025). The Pillow reference says save() takes the format from the file name extension when none is given, the format page gives the JPEG option quality a default of 75, and the PNG specification defines the pHYs chunk for pixel density (Pillow Developers, 2026)(World Wide Web Consortium, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch21#S_SaveFormats> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-SaveFormats" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:ImageObject .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:SaveFormats .
pg:ImageEditing a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Image editing"@en ;
    skos:definition "The second part of the chapter, Manipulating Images with Pillow, edits an opened picture with a handful of methods: crop(), copy(), paste(), resize(), rotate() and transpose(), and getpixel() and putpixel() for single pixels (Sweigart, 2025). The summary lists the same six methods and save() (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch21#S_ImageEditing> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ImageEditing" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 1 .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ImageEditing .
pg:CropAndPaste a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Crop and paste"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter crops the face out of zophie.png, copies the picture, pastes the face twice, and tiles it with two nested loops, noting that crop() returns a new image and paste() changes its own (Sweigart, 2025). The Pillow reference describes both calls (Pillow Developers, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch21#S_CropAndPaste> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-CropAndPaste" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:ImageEditing .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:CropAndPaste .
pg:CropBox a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Crop box"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says crop() takes a box tuple, returns a new Image and leaves the original untouched, and crops the face with (335, 345, 565, 560) (Sweigart, 2025). Appendix B answer 6 gives im.crop((0, 50, 50, 50)) for a quarter of a picture (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch21#S_CropBox> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-CropBox" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:CropAndPaste .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:CropBox .
pg:PasteInPlace a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Paste in place"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says copy() returns a new Image so the original can be kept, that paste() modifies its Image in place and does not return one, and that copy() and paste() do not use the clipboard (Sweigart, 2025). The Pillow reference gives the paste box as a 2-tuple for the upper left corner and says a pasted image of another mode is converted to the mode of the target (Pillow Developers, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch21#S_PasteInPlace> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-PasteInPlace" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:CropAndPaste .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:PasteInPlace .
pg:PasteMask a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Paste mask"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says an image with transparency must be passed as the optional third argument, otherwise its transparent pixels appear as white pixels, and it shows both results in Figure 21-13 (Sweigart, 2025). The Pillow reference says a mask of mode 1, L, LA, RGBA or RGBa updates only the regions it indicates, that the alpha band is used as the mask if present, and that where the mask is 0 the current value is kept (Pillow Developers, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch21#S_PasteMask> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-PasteMask" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:CropAndPaste .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:PasteMask .
pg:TileLoop a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Tile loop"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter tiles the 230 by 215 face over the 816 by 1088 picture with a loop over left in range(0, width, face width) and an inner loop over top, and prints the positions from (0, 0) to (690, 1075) (Sweigart, 2025). Nothing in the chapter says what happens at the right and bottom edge, where the last tiles do not fit."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch21#S_TileLoop> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-TileLoop" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:CropAndPaste .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:TileLoop .
pg:ResizeAndRotate a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Resize and rotate"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter resizes the cat to half its width and height and to a taller shape, and rotates it by 90, 180, 270 and 6 degrees, with and without expand (Sweigart, 2025). The Pillow reference documents resize() with a resample argument and rotate() with expand and fillcolor (Pillow Developers, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch21#S_ResizeAndRotate> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ResizeAndRotate" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:ImageEditing .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ResizeAndRotate .
pg:ResizeInteger a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Resize integer"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says resize() accepts only integers in its tuple, which is why both divisions are wrapped in int(), and that it returns a new Image (Sweigart, 2025). The Pillow reference gives size as the requested size in pixels, (width, height) (Pillow Developers, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch21#S_ResizeInteger> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ResizeInteger" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:ResizeAndRotate .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ResizeInteger .
pg:ResizeFilter a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Resize filter"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter does not name a resampling filter and calls resize() with a size only (Sweigart, 2025). The Pillow reference says the default filter is Resampling.BICUBIC, except for modes 1 and P, which always use NEAREST, and lists NEAREST, BOX, BILINEAR, HAMMING, BICUBIC and LANCZOS (Pillow Developers, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch21#S_ResizeFilter> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ResizeFilter" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:ResizeAndRotate .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ResizeFilter .
pg:RotateFill a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Rotate fill"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says rotated images keep their size, that on Windows a black background fills the gaps and that on macOS and Linux transparent pixels fill them instead (Sweigart, 2025). The Pillow reference describes fillcolor of rotate() as an optional colour for the area outside the rotated image and names no operating system (Pillow Developers, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch21#S_RotateFill> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-RotateFill" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:ResizeAndRotate .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:RotateFill .
pg:RotateExpand a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Rotate expand"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says expand=True enlarges the image to fit the whole rotated picture and that with 90, 180 or 270 degrees the result has no black or transparent background (Sweigart, 2025). The Pillow reference describes expand as making the output image large enough to hold the entire rotated image (Pillow Developers, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch21#S_RotateExpand> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-RotateExpand" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:ResizeAndRotate .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:RotateExpand .
pg:FlipAndPixels a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Flip and pixels"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter mirrors the picture with transpose() and the constants Image.FLIP_LEFT_RIGHT and Image.FLIP_TOP_BOTTOM, and reads and sets single pixels with getpixel() and putpixel(), colouring half a 100 by 100 image grey (Sweigart, 2025). The Pillow release notes of 9.1.0 moved the constants to Image.Transpose and those of 9.4.0 restored the old names (Pillow Developers, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch21#S_FlipAndPixels> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-FlipAndPixels" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:ImageEditing .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:FlipAndPixels .
pg:TransposeFlip a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Transpose flip"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter flips horizontally with Image.FLIP_LEFT_RIGHT and vertically with Image.FLIP_TOP_BOTTOM and says transpose() returns a new Image (Sweigart, 2025). Pillow 9.1.0 deprecated the module constants in favour of Image.Transpose and 9.4.0 reversed that, so both spellings work (Pillow Developers, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch21#S_TransposeFlip> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-TransposeFlip" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:FlipAndPixels .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:TransposeFlip .
pg:PixelAccess a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Pixel access"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says getpixel() and putpixel() take an x and y tuple, that putpixel() accepts an RGBA or an RGB tuple, and that a colour name such as 'darkgray' has to go through ImageColor.getcolor() (Sweigart, 2025). The Pillow reference says of putpixel() that it is relatively slow and points to paste() or the ImageDraw module for larger changes (Pillow Developers, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch21#S_PixelAccess> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-PixelAccess" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:FlipAndPixels .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:PixelAccess .
pg:LogoProject a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Logo project"@en ;
    skos:definition "Project 16, Add a Logo, is the chapter's one complete program: it resizes every .png and .jpg file of the working directory to fit a 300 by 300 square, pastes catlogo.png into the bottom-right corner and saves the result in a folder named withLogo, in four steps that open the logo, loop over the files, resize and paste (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch21#S_LogoProject> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-LogoProject" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 1 .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:LogoProject .
pg:LogoSetup a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Logo setup"@en ;
    skos:definition "Step 1 and Step 2 put two constants at the top, open the logo once and loop over os.listdir('.') with an if statement that skips names without .png or .jpg and the logo itself, using continue (Sweigart, 2025). The Python documentation says os.listdir() returns the names in arbitrary order (Python Software Foundation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch21#S_LogoSetup> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-LogoSetup" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:LogoProject .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:LogoSetup .
pg:LogoConstants a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Logo constants"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says setting SQUARE_FIT_SIZE and LOGO_FILENAME at the start makes the program easy to change, and that without them a reader would have to search the code for every 300 and 'catlogo.png' (Sweigart, 2025). It adds that command line arguments could set them (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch21#S_LogoConstants> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-LogoConstants" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:LogoSetup .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:LogoConstants .
pg:LogoFileLoop a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Logo file loop"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter loops over os.listdir('.'), skips names that do not end with .png or .jpg and the logo's name, and says the loop uses continue to go to the next file (Sweigart, 2025). The Python os documentation says listdir returns the names in arbitrary order (Python Software Foundation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch21#S_LogoFileLoop> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-LogoFileLoop" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:LogoSetup .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:LogoFileLoop .
pg:LogoResizeLogic a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Logo resize logic"@en ;
    skos:definition "Step 3 resizes only if the width and the height are both larger than 300, and then scales the longer side to 300 and the other side by the same proportion, converting the float result with int() because resize() needs integers (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch21#S_LogoResizeLogic> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-LogoResizeLogic" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:LogoProject .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:LogoResizeLogic .
pg:BothSidesTest a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Both sides test"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter's list of what the program does says to check whether the image is wider and taller than 300 pixels, and the code tests width > SQUARE_FIT_SIZE and height > SQUARE_FIT_SIZE with and (Sweigart, 2025). The chapter's own summary calls the aim fitting every picture into 300 by 300 (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch21#S_BothSidesTest> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-BothSidesTest" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:LogoResizeLogic .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:BothSidesTest .
pg:ProportionMath a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Proportion math"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter multiplies the ratio SQUARE_FIT_SIZE / width by the height for a wide picture and the ratio SQUARE_FIT_SIZE / height by the width otherwise, and says int() must convert the float (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch21#S_ProportionMath> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ProportionMath" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:LogoResizeLogic .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ProportionMath .
pg:LogoPlacement a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Logo placement"@en ;
    skos:definition "Step 4 pastes the logo at the image width minus the logo width and the image height minus the logo height, passes the logo as the third argument, prints a message, and saves under the original filename in withLogo (Sweigart, 2025). Figure 21-12 draws the arithmetic and Figure 21-13 shows the result with and without the mask (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch21#S_LogoPlacement> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-LogoPlacement" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:LogoProject .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:LogoPlacement .
pg:CornerPosition a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Corner position"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says the left coordinate is the image width minus the logo width and the top coordinate is the image height minus the logo height, as Figure 21-12 shows (Sweigart, 2025). The Pillow reference takes the 2-tuple of paste() as the upper left corner of the pasted image (Pillow Developers, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch21#S_CornerPosition> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-CornerPosition" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:LogoPlacement .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:CornerPosition .
pg:MaskedPaste a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Masked paste"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says paste() does not paste the transparency pixels unless logo_im is passed as the third argument, and Figure 21-13 shows the transparent pixels as solid white without it (Sweigart, 2025). The Pillow reference says the alpha band of the mask decides what is copied, 255 as it is and 0 left alone (Pillow Developers, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch21#S_MaskedPaste> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-MaskedPaste" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:LogoPlacement .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:MaskedPaste .
pg:OutputFolder a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Output folder"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says os.makedirs() creates withLogo instead of overwriting the originals and that exist_ok=True keeps it from raising if the folder exists, and the program saves each file under its old name (Sweigart, 2025). The Python documentation says that with exist_ok False, the default, FileExistsError is raised if the target directory exists (Python Software Foundation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch21#S_OutputFolder> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-OutputFolder" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:LogoPlacement .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:OutputFolder .
pg:ShippedScript a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Shipped script"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter prints the program in four listings and says the file can be had from the online materials; its fourth listing writes (width – logo_width, height – logo_height) with an en dash (Sweigart, 2025). The file in the publisher's zip is the same program with an ASCII minus and a header comment (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch21#S_ShippedScript> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ShippedScript" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:LogoPlacement .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ShippedScript .
pg:DrawingAndClipboard a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Drawing and clipboard"@en ;
    skos:definition "The third part of the chapter draws on images with the ImageDraw module, which has methods for points, lines, rectangles, ellipses and polygons and for text, and then copies and pastes images with pyperclipimg, an operating-system clipboard tool (Sweigart, 2025). Its drawing example makes the 200 by 200 picture of Figure 21-14 (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch21#S_DrawingAndClipboard> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-DrawingAndClipboard" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 1 .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:DrawingAndClipboard .
pg:ImageDrawShapes a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Image draw shapes"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter lists point(), line(), rectangle(), ellipse() and polygon() with their fill, outline and width arguments, says the optional fill and outline default to white, and draws a 200 by 200 example (Sweigart, 2025). The Pillow ImageDraw page documents the same methods and the width argument (Pillow Developers, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch21#S_ImageDrawShapes> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ImageDrawShapes" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:DrawingAndClipboard .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ImageDrawShapes .
pg:DrawObject a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Draw object"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter passes the Image object to ImageDraw.Draw() and stores the ImageDraw object in draw so the following examples can use it (Sweigart, 2025). The Pillow ImageDraw page says the module provides simple 2D graphics for Image objects (Pillow Developers, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch21#S_DrawObject> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-DrawObject" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:ImageDrawShapes .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:DrawObject .
pg:PointsAndLines a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Points and lines"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says point() draws individual pixels from a list of tuples or of bare coordinates and notes that point here means a pixel, not the unit of font size; line() draws one or more lines and takes a width that defaults to 1 (Sweigart, 2025). The Pillow page documents both (Pillow Developers, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch21#S_PointsAndLines> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-PointsAndLines" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:ImageDrawShapes .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:PointsAndLines .
pg:RectangleEllipse a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Rectangle ellipse"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says the box of rectangle() and ellipse() is a left, top, right, bottom tuple, that equal width and height make a circle, and that fill and outline default to white (Sweigart, 2025). Unlike crop(), where it says the right column is not included, it gives no rule for ImageDraw (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch21#S_RectangleEllipse> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-RectangleEllipse" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:ImageDrawShapes .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:RectangleEllipse .
pg:PolygonDrawing a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Polygon drawing"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says polygon() takes tuples or integers for the connecting points, that the last pair of coordinates is joined to the first, and that fill and outline are optional (Sweigart, 2025). The Pillow page documents polygon() with the same arguments (Pillow Developers, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch21#S_PolygonDrawing> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-PolygonDrawing" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:ImageDrawShapes .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:PolygonDrawing .
pg:DrawingExample a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Drawing example"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter draws a black border, a blue rectangle, a red ellipse, a brown five-point polygon and, in a loop from 100 to 190 in steps of 10, green lines from (i, 0) to (200, i - 100), and shows the result as Figure 21-14 (Sweigart, 2025). The picture is 200 by 200 (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch21#S_DrawingExample> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-DrawingExample" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:ImageDrawShapes .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:DrawingExample .
pg:TextDrawing a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Text drawing"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter draws text with draw.text(), takes a font from ImageFont.truetype() with the file name and a size it calls points, and says Pillow's PNGs are 72 pixels per inch so a point is one pixel (Sweigart, 2025). The Pillow ImageFont page says the size is in pixels (Pillow Developers, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch21#S_TextDrawing> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-TextDrawing" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:DrawingAndClipboard .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:TextDrawing .
pg:DefaultFont a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Default font"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter writes Hello with no font argument and says the typeface and size are not customised (Sweigart, 2025). The Pillow release notes of 10.1.0 say a version of Aileron Regular that can be drawn at chosen sizes is now loaded, and the reference says load_default() takes a size (Pillow Developers, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch21#S_DefaultFont> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-DefaultFont" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:TextDrawing .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:DefaultFont .
pg:TrueTypeFont a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "True type font"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter calls ImageFont.truetype('arial.ttf', 32) and says Pillow searches C:\\Windows\\Fonts, /Library/Fonts, /System/Library/Fonts and /usr/share/fonts/truetype and raises an error for a font it cannot find (Sweigart, 2025). The Pillow page lists Windows, macOS and Linux folders, among them /usr/share/fonts, and says the size is the requested size in pixels (Pillow Developers, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch21#S_TrueTypeFont> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-TrueTypeFont" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:TextDrawing .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:TrueTypeFont .
pg:TextMeasuring a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Text measuring"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter does not measure text; it draws Howdy at (100, 150) on a 200 pixel image and shows Figure 21-15 (Sweigart, 2025). The Pillow ImageDraw page documents textbbox() and textlength(), and the release notes of 10.0.0 say textsize() and getsize() were removed in favour of them (Pillow Developers, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch21#S_TextMeasuring> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-TextMeasuring" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:TextDrawing .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:TextMeasuring .
pg:ClipboardAndViewer a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Clipboard and viewer"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter's show() opens the picture in a window for debugging, and the section on the clipboard says pyperclipimg.copy() puts a Pillow Image on the operating system's clipboard and paste() returns the clipboard's image (Sweigart, 2025). The pyperclipimg README names xclip or wl-copy as the programs it needs on Linux (Sweigart, 2024)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch21#S_ClipboardAndViewer> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ClipboardAndViewer" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:DrawingAndClipboard .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ClipboardAndViewer .
pg:ShowViewer a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Show viewer"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says show() opens the image in a window and is useful when debugging (Sweigart, 2025). The Pillow ImageShow page lists XDGViewer, which runs the freedesktop.org xdg-open command, and DisplayViewer, the ImageMagick display command, among the Unix viewers registered if their command is found, and says the temporary files created from images are not removed (Pillow Developers, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch21#S_ShowViewer> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ShowViewer" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:ClipboardAndViewer .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ShowViewer .
pg:ClipboardCopyPaste a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Clipboard copy paste"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter copies zophie.png with pyperclipimg.copy(im), pastes it with pyperclipimg.paste() and shows it, and says the module lets users put image data into and out of Python programs (Sweigart, 2025). The README of pyperclipimg says it needs xclip or wl-copy on Linux and PyPI lists version 0.2.0 (Sweigart, 2024)(Python Package Index, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch21#S_ClipboardCopyPaste> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ClipboardCopyPaste" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:ClipboardAndViewer .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ClipboardCopyPaste .
pg:ClipboardFailures a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Clipboard failures"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter shows only the working case and tells the reader to copy a new image from a graphics program before paste() (Sweigart, 2025). The README says the module needs xclip or wl-copy on Linux (Sweigart, 2024)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch21#S_ClipboardFailures> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ClipboardFailures" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:ClipboardAndViewer .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ClipboardFailures .
pg:MatplotlibGraphs a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Matplotlib graphs"@en ;
    skos:definition "The last part of the chapter makes graphs with Matplotlib: a line graph and a scatter plot of the same two series, a bar graph and a pie chart, and then markers, colours, labels and a legend, saving with plt.savefig() and previewing with plt.show() (Sweigart, 2025). The Matplotlib interface guide calls pyplot an implicit interface that keeps track of the last Figure and Axes created (Matplotlib Development Team, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch21#S_MatplotlibGraphs> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-MatplotlibGraphs" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 1 .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:MatplotlibGraphs .
pg:FigureModel a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Figure model"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter calls plt a short name for matplotlib.pyplot, says a figure is the window that holds one or more plots and that Matplotlib picks colours and size by itself (Sweigart, 2025). The Matplotlib guide to its interfaces says pyplot keeps a list of figures and that gcf and gca return the current figure and axes (Matplotlib Development Team, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch21#S_FigureModel> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-FigureModel" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:MatplotlibGraphs .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:FigureModel .
pg:PyplotState a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Pyplot state"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter calls plt.plot() twice to put two lines on one graph, saves with savefig() and calls plt.show() (Sweigart, 2025). The Matplotlib interface guide calls pyplot an implicit interface that keeps track of the last Figure and Axes created and adds artists to the object it thinks the user wants (Matplotlib Development Team, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch21#S_PyplotState> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-PyplotState" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:FigureModel .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:PyplotState .
pg:FigureDefaults a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Figure defaults"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says Matplotlib selects colours for the lines and an appropriate size for the graph automatically, and does not give the size (Sweigart, 2025). The Matplotlib savefig reference documents the dpi argument (Matplotlib Development Team, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch21#S_FigureDefaults> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-FigureDefaults" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:FigureModel .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:FigureDefaults .
pg:BackendChoice a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Backend choice"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says plt.show() opens a window with the graph and that the window is interactive, and says nothing about a program that has no window (Sweigart, 2025). The Matplotlib backends guide separates interactive backends such as TkAgg from non-interactive ones such as Agg (Matplotlib Development Team, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch21#S_BackendChoice> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-BackendChoice" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:FigureModel .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:BackendChoice .
pg:LineAndScatter a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Line and scatter"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says a line graph is ideal for showing changes in one measure over time and makes it with plt.plot() twice, then makes the scatter plot of the same data with plt.scatter(), noting that the code is identical except for the function name (Sweigart, 2025). The Matplotlib pages document both functions (Matplotlib Development Team, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch21#S_LineAndScatter> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-LineAndScatter" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:MatplotlibGraphs .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:LineAndScatter .
pg:LinePlot a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Line plot"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter plots x_values against y_values1 and y_values2 and shows the shell returning a list for each call, and the first values of the two lists belong together (Sweigart, 2025). The Matplotlib reference says plot() returns a list of Line2D objects (Matplotlib Development Team, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch21#S_LinePlot> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-LinePlot" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:LineAndScatter .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:LinePlot .
pg:ScatterPlot a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Scatter plot"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says the scatter plot uses points instead of connected lines and that the data are the same as in the line graph (Sweigart, 2025). The Matplotlib reference says scatter() returns a PathCollection (Matplotlib Development Team, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch21#S_ScatterPlot> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ScatterPlot" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:LineAndScatter .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ScatterPlot .
pg:BarAndPie a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Bar and pie"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says a bar graph compares the same data in different categories, that unlike a line graph the order of categories is not important, and that a pie chart has labels and slices and an autopct format string (Sweigart, 2025). The Matplotlib pages for bar() and pie() document both (Matplotlib Development Team, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch21#S_BarAndPie> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-BarAndPie" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:MatplotlibGraphs .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:BarAndPie .
pg:BarChart a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Bar chart"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter calls plt.bar(categories, values) with the four categories Cats, Dogs, Mice and Moose and the values 100 to 400 and says the order of categories is not important (Sweigart, 2025). The Matplotlib reference says bar() returns a BarContainer and accepts categorical x values (Matplotlib Development Team, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch21#S_BarChart> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-BarChart" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:BarAndPie .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:BarChart .
pg:PieChart a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Pie chart"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter calls plt.pie(slices, labels=labels, autopct='%.1f%%') and shows a printed tuple, and says that without autopct the chart will not list the percentage text (Sweigart, 2025). The Matplotlib reference says that since 3.11 pie() returns a PieContainer and that the wedges and texts were returned in a tuple before (Matplotlib Development Team, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch21#S_PieChart> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-PieChart" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:BarAndPie .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:PieChart .
pg:DecorationsAndOutput a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Decorations and output"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter adds marker, color and label keywords, calls plt.legend(), plt.xlabel(), plt.ylabel(), plt.title() and plt.grid(True), and says to call plt.savefig() before plt.show() (Sweigart, 2025). The Matplotlib savefig reference gives dpi the default 'figure', the dpi of the figure, and documents format (Matplotlib Development Team, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch21#S_DecorationsAndOutput> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-DecorationsAndOutput" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:MatplotlibGraphs .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:DecorationsAndOutput .
pg:PlotDecorations a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Plot decorations"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter's example gives the lines marker='o' with color='b' and marker='s' with color='r', labels them, adds a legend and the three labels, and prints Text(0.5, 0, 'X-axis Label'), Text(0, 0.5, 'Y-axis Label') and Text(0.5, 1.0, 'Graph Title') (Sweigart, 2025). The Matplotlib reference for plot() lists the marker and color codes (Matplotlib Development Team, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch21#S_PlotDecorations> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-PlotDecorations" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:DecorationsAndOutput .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:PlotDecorations .
pg:SaveFigure a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Save figure"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter saves with plt.savefig('linegraph.png') and says the call must come before plt.show() (Sweigart, 2025). The Matplotlib reference says dpi defaults to the figure's dpi value and format names the file format (Matplotlib Development Team, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch21#S_SaveFigure> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-SaveFigure" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:DecorationsAndOutput .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:SaveFigure .
pg:ShowLifecycle a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Show lifecycle"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says plt.show() blocks until the window is closed, that closing it resets the graph data so a second plt.show() does nothing or shows an empty window, and that plt.savefig() must come first (Sweigart, 2025). Appendix B answer 12 says you cannot call plt.show() twice in a row because it resets the graph data (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch21#S_ShowLifecycle> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ShowLifecycle" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:DecorationsAndOutput .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ShowLifecycle .
pg:BookAndPractice a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Book and practice"@en ;
    skos:definition "The rest of the chapter is the pins and files around the text: Appendix A names the packages to install, the summary and the 12 practice questions check what was learned, and three practice programs, Tile Maker, the photo folder finder and the seating cards, use the files of the online materials (Sweigart, 2025). Appendix B gives the answers (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch21#S_BookAndPractice> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-BookAndPractice" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 1 .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:BookAndPractice .
pg:InstallAndVersions a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Install and versions"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter tells the reader to install Pillow, Matplotlib and pyperclipimg by Appendix A, which pins pillow==10.4.0 and matplotlib==3.92 and lists pyperclipimg with no number (Sweigart, 2025). PyPI lists the releases and the Python each one supports (Python Package Index, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch21#S_InstallAndVersions> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-InstallAndVersions" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:BookAndPractice .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:InstallAndVersions .
pg:AppendixPins a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Appendix pins"@en ;
    skos:definition "Appendix A lists pillow==10.4.0, matplotlib==3.92 and requests==2.32.3 and does not number pyperclipimg (Sweigart, 2025). PyPI shows the releases of Matplotlib as plain numbers such as 3.9.2, 3.10.0 and 3.11.2 (Python Package Index, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch21#S_AppendixPins> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-AppendixPins" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:InstallAndVersions .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:AppendixPins .
pg:PythonWheelSupport a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Python wheel support"@en ;
    skos:definition "The Pillow page on Python support lists the Python versions each release supports, and its release notes of 12.0.0 say Pillow 11.3.0 had wheels for the 3.14 beta and Pillow 12.0.0 officially supports Python 3.14 (Pillow Developers, 2026). PyPI lists the files of each release, and a wheel is built for one interpreter series; for a release without one the installer builds from source, as it did here (Python Package Index, 2026)(This study, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch21#S_PythonWheelSupport> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-PythonWheelSupport" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:InstallAndVersions .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:PythonWheelSupport .
pg:PinnedBuildOnSource a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Pinned build on source"@en ;
    skos:definition "The book pins Matplotlib as 3.92, meaning 3.9.2, and the Pillow page of Python support says Pillow 12 supports Python 3.14 (Pillow Developers, 2026). Matplotlib's dependency page lists Tk (>= 8.5, != 8.6.0 or 8.6.1) for the Tk-based backends, a rule with no upper limit (Matplotlib Development Team, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch21#S_PinnedBuildOnSource> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-PinnedBuildOnSource" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:InstallAndVersions .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:PinnedBuildOnSource .
pg:TkVersionNine a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Tk version nine"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter's windows are Tk windows, which tkinter provides; the Python documentation of tkinter writes the Tcl/Tk version in the notation '9.0.3' and says Tk 9.0 added window attributes (Python Software Foundation, 2026), and the Tcl project publishes Tcl and Tk 9.0 (Tcl Developer Exchange, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch21#S_TkVersionNine> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-TkVersionNine" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:InstallAndVersions .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:TkVersionNine .
pg:BookCorrections a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Book corrections"@en ;
    skos:definition "Several statements of the chapter and its appendices do not hold when they are run: a table value, the pixel density of a PNG, the fill of a rotated corner, three answers of Appendix B and the files said to be in the online resources (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch21#S_BookCorrections> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-BookCorrections" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:BookAndPractice .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:BookCorrections .
pg:GreenTableError a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Green table error"@en ;
    skos:definition "Table 21-1 lists eight names and says they are standard HTML colour names, among them Green as (0, 255, 0, 255) and Gray as (128, 128, 128, 255) (Sweigart, 2025). The CSS Color module lists green as #008000, which is 0 128 0, and lime as #00ff00, which is 0 255 0 (World Wide Web Consortium, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch21#S_GreenTableError> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-GreenTableError" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:BookCorrections .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:GreenTableError .
pg:DpiClaim a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Dpi claim"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says Pillow creates PNG images that are 72 pixels per inch by default and that a 4 by 5 inch card would require 288 by 360 pixels (Sweigart, 2025). The Pillow format page documents the dpi option of the PNG writer and the PNG specification defines the pHYs chunk (Pillow Developers, 2026)(World Wide Web Consortium, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch21#S_DpiClaim> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-DpiClaim" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:BookCorrections .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:DpiClaim .
pg:RotateFillClaim a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Rotate fill claim"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says that on Windows a black background fills the gaps of a rotation and that on macOS and Linux transparent pixels fill them instead (Sweigart, 2025). Pillow's reference for rotate() names a fill colour for the area outside and no operating system (Pillow Developers, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch21#S_RotateFillClaim> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-RotateFillClaim" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:BookCorrections .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:RotateFillClaim .
pg:AppendixBErrors a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Appendix b errors"@en ;
    skos:definition "Appendix B answers question 3 with left, top, width and height, question 6 with im.crop((0, 50, 50, 50)) for the lower-left quarter of a 100 by 100 image, and question 12 with the statement that plt.show() resets the graph data so it cannot be called twice (Sweigart, 2025). The chapter itself defines the box as left, top, right and bottom (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch21#S_AppendixBErrors> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-AppendixBErrors" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:BookCorrections .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:AppendixBErrors .
pg:MissingFiles a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Missing files"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter points to the book's online resources for zophie.png, catlogo.png and guests.txt, shows output for zophie_xmas_tree.png and me_and_zophie.png, uses zophie_the_cat.jpg in a practice program and says a public domain flower image is also available (Sweigart, 2025). The book page links the zip (No Starch Press, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch21#S_MissingFiles> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-MissingFiles" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:BookCorrections .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:MissingFiles .
pg:DeprecatedApis a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Deprecated apis"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter uses neither textsize() nor getsize(), which the 2nd edition's chapter 19 used (Sweigart, 2025)(Sweigart, 2019). The Pillow deprecation page lists what is removed and what is deprecated and when (Pillow Developers, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch21#S_DeprecatedApis> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-DeprecatedApis" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:BookAndPractice .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:DeprecatedApis .
pg:TextsizeGone a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Textsize gone"@en ;
    skos:definition "The 2nd edition's chapter 19 mentions Pillow 30 times, ImageFont 13 times and textsize 3 times, and neither matplotlib nor pyperclipimg, and the Pillow release notes of 10.0.0 say ImageDraw.textsize() and ImageFont.getsize() were removed in favour of textbbox(), textlength() and getbbox() (Sweigart, 2019)(Pillow Developers, 2026). The 3rd edition's chapter does not use them (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch21#S_TextsizeGone> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-TextsizeGone" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:DeprecatedApis .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:TextsizeGone .
pg:GetdataDeprecation a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Getdata deprecation"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter does not use getdata(), and the Pillow deprecation page says getdata() has been deprecated since 12.1.0 and that get_flattened_data() can be used instead (Pillow Developers, 2026). This study's own programs used getdata() to count pixels (This study, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch21#S_GetdataDeprecation> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-GetdataDeprecation" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:DeprecatedApis .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:GetdataDeprecation .
pg:PracticePrograms a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Practice programs"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter's three practice programs are Tile Maker, which makes a larger image from one tile with make_tile(), the photo folder finder, which walks the hard drive for folders that are mostly large pictures, and the seating cards for the guests of guests.txt (Sweigart, 2025). The chapter says guests.txt and a flower image are among the book's online resources (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch21#S_PracticePrograms> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-PracticePrograms" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:BookAndPractice .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:PracticePrograms .
pg:TileMaker a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Tile maker"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter asks for make_tile() with a string filename and two integers for the number of tiles across and down, and says a 20 by 50 pixel zophie_the_cat.jpg tiled 6 by 10 should give a 120 by 500 image with 60 tiles (Sweigart, 2025). Its bonus asks for random flips or rotations (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch21#S_TileMaker> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-TileMaker" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:PracticePrograms .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:TileMaker .
pg:PhotoFolderFinder a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Photo folder finder"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter defines a photo folder as one where more than half of the files are photos, a photo as a .png or .jpg file whose width and height are both larger than 500 pixels, and gives a skeleton around os.walk('C:\\\\') (Sweigart, 2025). The Python os documentation describes os.walk (Python Software Foundation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch21#S_PhotoFolderFinder> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-PhotoFolderFinder" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:PracticePrograms .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:PhotoFolderFinder .
pg:SeatingCards a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Seating cards"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter asks for one image for each guest of guests.txt with the name and some flowery decoration and a black rectangle at the edges, and says Pillow's PNGs are 72 pixels per inch so a 4 by 5 inch card is 288 by 360 pixels (Sweigart, 2025). It points to a public domain flower image in the online resources (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch21#S_SeatingCards> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-SeatingCards" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:PracticePrograms .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:SeatingCards .
